"""Class 10 SST Ch 11 Manufacturing Industries (Geography) - extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4(r"Manufacturing is regarded as the backbone of economic development.", r"It helps modernise agriculture, reduces dependence on agriculture by providing jobs in secondary and tertiary sectors and brings in foreign exchange through exports.", "a",
     r"These functions of the sector are why it is called the backbone; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"The iron and steel industry is called a basic or key industry.", r"Bhilai, Durgapur, Rourkela and Bokaro are steel plants in the public sector.", "b",
     r"Both true; R is not the reason for the 'basic' label."),
 AR4(r"The sugar industry is shifting towards the southern and western states.", r"The cane grown in India's northern states has a higher sucrose content.", "c",
     r"A is true. Cane in the south and Maharashtra has higher sucrose content, so R is false."),
 AR4(r"Industrial pollution is caused only by air pollution.", r"Thermal pollution of water occurs when hot water from factories and thermal plants is drained into rivers and ponds before cooling.", "d",
     r"Industries cause air, water, noise and land pollution, so A is false; R is true."),
 AR4(r"The jute industry is concentrated in the Hugli basin of West Bengal.", r"The area has jute growing nearby, cheap water transport, abundant water for processing and cheap labour.", "a",
     r"These factors favour the mills' location; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Mumbai was the site of the first successful modern cotton textile mill in 1854.", r"Ahmedabad is also a major centre of the cotton textile industry.", "b",
     r"Both true; they are separate facts and R does not explain A."),
]

CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> A paper mill beside a river in Uttar Pradesh releases hot water, chemicals and solid waste into the river. Local people report fish deaths downstream. Industries also cause air pollution from smoke and gases, noise pollution from machines, generators and drilling, and land pollution from solid wastes such as fly ash and slag. "
   r"(i) Name the type of pollution that occurs when hot water is discharged into rivers. (ii) Name one solid waste mentioned. (iii) Give one source of noise pollution. (iv) Suggest two steps the mill should take to reduce water pollution.",
   r"(i) Thermal pollution (1). (ii) Fly ash or slag (1). (iii) Machinery, generators or drilling (any one) (1). (iv) Treat effluents before discharge and recycle water; cool hot water before release; use rainwater harvesting for processing (any two) (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> The iron and steel industry is a basic industry since all other industries depend on it for their machinery. Its raw materials are iron ore, coking coal, limestone and manganese. The plants at Bhilai, Durgapur, Rourkela and Bokaro are public-sector units run by SAIL, while the Tata Iron and Steel Company is a private-sector unit located at Jamshedpur. "
   r"(i) Why is the iron and steel industry called a basic industry? (ii) Name two raw materials of the industry. (iii) Name the state in which Jamshedpur is located. (iv) Why are steel plants located near iron ore and coal fields?",
   r"(i) Other industries use its products as raw material for machines and tools (1). (ii) Any two of iron ore, coking coal, limestone, manganese (1). (iii) Jharkhand (1). (iv) Iron ore and coal are bulky and heavy and lose weight in processing, so transport cost is reduced by locating near them (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> The sugar industry is seasonal in nature. Its raw material, sugarcane, is bulky and its sucrose content reduces during haulage. Mills were earlier concentrated in Uttar Pradesh and Bihar, but the industry has shifted to southern and western states, especially Maharashtra, where cane has higher sucrose content, the cooler climate ensures a longer crushing season and cooperatives are more successful. "
   r"(i) Why is the sugar industry seasonal? (ii) Which state is a leading centre of the shift? (iii) What reduces during haulage of sugarcane? (iv) Why are sugar mills located close to cane fields?",
   r"(i) Sugarcane is available only in a particular season (1). (ii) Maharashtra (1). (iii) Sucrose content (1). (iv) Cane is bulky and loses its sucrose content if transported far or kept long, so mills near the fields reduce losses and costs (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

